{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1480 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1945897c885b676344e6d871541db1f4700e57426b789ce15f7442730130ae50" :edb/db-before-t 1479 :edb/db-after-t 1480 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534792 50 #inst "2026-10-12T14:07:10.986Z" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186089513 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089516 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186089549 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089551 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186091925 1071 "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B$, and $C$. There are $7$ female workers in department $A$, while there are $8$ female workers in department $C$. What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B$?" 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186091925 1071 "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B,$ and $C.$ There are $7$ female workers in department $A,$ while there are $8$ female workers in department $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B?$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186091925 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C)\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)$:\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A)$, we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40)$. So, we have\n$P(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}$.\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C)$, we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40)$. So, we have\n$P(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}$.\n- Also, we're given that\n$P(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}$.\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(F) &= P(F∩A) + P(F∩B) + P(F∩C) \\\\\n\\frac{21}{40} &= \\frac{7}{40} + P(F∩B) + \\frac{8}{40} \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{21}{40} - \\frac{7}{40} - \\frac{8}{40}. \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{6}{40} \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{3}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186091925 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A),$ we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C),$ we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap A)+P(F\\cap B)+P(F\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{21}{40} & =\\frac{7}{40}+P(F\\cap B)+\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{21}{40}-\\frac{7}{40}-\\frac{8}{40}. \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{6}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186091926 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A}$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B}$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D∩A) + P(D∩B) + P(D∩C) \\\\\n&= P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid B)P(B) + P(D \\mid C)P(C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n$P(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35$.\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A}$, $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B}$, and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have\n$P(D \\mid A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D \\mid B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D \\mid C) = 0.014$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid B)P(B) + P(D \\mid C)P(C) \\\\\n&= (0.015)(0.32) + (0.02)(0.33) + (0.014)(0.35) \\\\\n&= 0.0048 + 0.0066 + 0.0049 \\\\\n&= 0.0163\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186091926 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A},$ $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B},$ and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D | A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D | B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D | C) = 0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.015)(0.32)+(0.02)(0.33)+(0.014)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.004\\,8+0.006\\,6+0.004\\,9 \\\\  & =0.016\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186091927 1071 "Three baskets, $A, B$, and $C$, contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13$, what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186091927 1071 "Three baskets, $A, B,$ and $C,$ contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13,$ what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186091927 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A}$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B}$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(R) &= P(R∩A) + P(R∩B) + P(R∩C) \\\\\n&= P(R \\mid A)P(A) + P(R \\mid B)P(B) + P(R \\mid C)P(C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n$P(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n$P(R \\mid A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R \\mid B) = \\dfrac14$.\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13$, we have\n$P(R) = \\dfrac13$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R \\mid C)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(R) &= P(R\\begin{vmatrix}A)P(A) + P(R \\mid B)P(B) + P(R \\mid C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}|= (\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3}) + (\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3}) + P(R \\mid C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}|= \\frac{2}{15} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C)|= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{2}{15} - \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C)|= \\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R\\end{vmatrix}C)|= \\frac{7}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{20}\n$$\n\nof the apples from basket $C$ are red." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186091927 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R | B) = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{7}{20}$ of the apples from basket $C$ are red." 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436222 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436226 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436229 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436234 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436225 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436227 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436228 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436231 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436233 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436223 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436224 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436230 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436232 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092453 1050 840.0 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092557 1071 "Alice has three email accounts.\nIn the first account, she received $16$ emails, of which $5$ were spam.\nIn the second account, she received $10$ emails, of which $2$ were spam.\nAnd in the third account, she received $14$ emails, of which $3$ were spam.\nBased on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email across all her accounts is spam?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186092557 1071 "Alice has three email accounts. \nIn the first account, she received $16$ emails, of which $5$ were spam. \nIn the second account, she received $10$ emails, of which $2$ were spam. \nAnd in the third account, she received $14$ emails, of which $3$ were spam. \nBased on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email across all her accounts is spam?" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092557 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that a chosen email is spam, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that Alice received a total of \n\n$$\n16+10+14=40\n$$\n\nemails in all three accounts.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{1}),$ we take the number of emails in the first account that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{1})=\\frac{5}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{2}),$ we take the number of emails in the second account that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{2})=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{3}),$ we take the number of emails in the third account that are spam $(3)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{3})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40}+\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092563 1071 "At a gas station, $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. It is known that $65%$ of the customers who purchase Regular gasoline and $50%$ of the customers who buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card. If the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%$, what percentage of Supreme gasoline customers pay using a credit card?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186092563 1071 "At a gas station, $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. It is known that $65%$ of the customers who purchase Regular gasoline and $50%$ of the customers who buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card. If the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%,$ what percentage of Supreme gasoline customers pay using a credit card?" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186092563 1074 "Let  $C$ be the event that the selected person pays using a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the customer bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the customer bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the customer bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected customer pays using a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the customers buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the customers buy Supreme gasoline, we have \n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the customers that buy Regular gasoline and  $50%$ of the customers that buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card, we have \n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(C)=0.575.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|S),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\ 0.575 & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+P(C|S)(0.15) \\\\ 0.575 & =0.26+0.225+0.15P(C|S) \\\\ 0.15P(C|S) & =0.09 \\\\ P(C|S) & =0.6=60%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60%$ of Supreme gasoline customers pay with a credit card." 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093201 1061 38126 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093201 1061 836329 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093202 1061 38127 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093202 1061 836331 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093203 1061 38129 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093203 1061 836334 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093204 1061 38130 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093204 1061 836335 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093205 1061 38132 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093205 1061 836338 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093206 1061 38958 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093206 1061 836330 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093475 1064 "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$, exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that\n\n- $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n- the events $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$, and we write\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S$. Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3$:\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + P(A∩B_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= ∑_(i)P(A∩B_{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots\n$$\n\nis a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093475 1064 "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that \n\n- $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n\n- the events $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of  $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ and we write \n \n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3.\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\n \n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S.$ Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\n \n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}P(A\\cap {B}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots$ is a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S.$" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093476 1064 "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) +  P(A\\cap B_2) +  P(A\\cap B_3),\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S.$\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities. \n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap {B}_{1}) & =P(A|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{2}) & =P(A|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) & =P(A|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|B_1)P(B_1)+  P(A|B_2)P(B_2) +  P(A|B_3)P(B_3).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186093476 1064 "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) + P(A\\cap B_2) + P(A\\cap B_3)\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities.\n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∩B_{1}) &= P(A\\begin{vmatrix}B_{1})P(B_{1}) \\\\ P(A∩B_{2})|= P(A \\mid B_{2})P(B_{2}) \\\\ P(A∩B_{3})|= P(A\\end{vmatrix}B_{3})P(B_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A \\mid B_1)P(B_1)+ P(A \\mid B_2)P(B_2) + P(A \\mid B_3)P(B_3)\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093477 1064 "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and\n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of\n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden.\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1)$, we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}$.\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2)$, we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}$.\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3)$, we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}$.\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + P(A∩B_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{36} + \\frac{6}{36} + \\frac{4}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36)$. However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534792 17592186045417 false] [17592186093477 1064 "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and \n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of \n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden. \n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1),$ we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2),$ we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3),$ we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{36}+\\frac{6}{36}+\\frac{4}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNote the following: \n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36).$ However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436144 1080 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436144 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436144 1083 #uuid "05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436145 1080 "0.27" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436145 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436145 1083 #uuid "0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436146 1080 "0.525" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436146 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436146 1083 #uuid "14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436147 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436147 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436147 1083 #uuid "18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436148 1080 "0.55" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436148 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436148 1083 #uuid "1feaa879-4a53-577a-ac18-96df41141043" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436149 1080 "0.29" 13194139534792 17592186045417 true] [17592186436149 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534792 1759218